Acute Severe Ulcerative Colitis
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Gastrointestinal Loss
6 bloody stools daily, Crampy abdominal pain/tenesmus, Continuous colonic mucosal inflammation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-003
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of frequent bloody diarrhea with systemic symptoms, requiring urgent after-hours assessment for severe dehydration and potential toxic megacolon.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a rapid deterioration with over six bloody bowel movements today, accompanied by dizziness. This presentation required urgent in-person evaluation to assess hemodynamic stability and fluid loss prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Constant bloody diarrhea and severe stomach cramps. Patient states, The cramps are agonizing and I can't stop rushing to the toilet; I feel incredibly weak.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms escalated progressively over the past three days. It began with mild lower abdominal discomfort and loose stools, rapidly evolving today into frequent bloody diarrhea exceeding six bowel movements per day. This is accompanied by severe crampy abdominal pain and tenesmus, providing no relief after defecation. The patient reports significant weight loss and anorexia over the past week. Systemic symptoms include a documented fever earlier today, intense fatigue, and dry mucous membranes. The patient reports postural hypotension, feeling dizzy and near-syncopal upon standing. Pallor secondary to presumed anemia is noted by the patient in the mirror.
Functional Status: Independent but currently restricted to the immediate vicinity of the bathroom due to fecal urgency and weakness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient is living alone, increasing the risk of unobserved syncope secondary to volume depletion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 mmHg | HR: 102 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 65.0 Kg
General: Appears pale, fatigued, holding abdomen, and visibly uncomfortable.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Mild tachycardia, normal heart sounds, weak radial pulses. Postural drop in blood pressure evident. Respiratory: Lungs clear to auscultation. Gastrointestinal: The clinical picture reflects the diagnostic triad of >6 bloody stools daily, severe crampy abdominal pain/tenesmus, and high suspicion for continuous colonic mucosal inflammation. The abdomen exhibits mild distension with marked tenderness, primarily in the left lower quadrant. No rebound tenderness or rigidity to suggest outright perforation. Rectal exam deferred to specialist setting but perianal skin shows excoriation. Integumentary: Pallor present, delayed capillary refill (3 seconds), and dry oral mucous membranes indicating moderate gastrointestinal fluid loss.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment. Bathroom is immediately adjacent to the bedroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient has been non-compliant with maintenance Mesalazine over the last month.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Severe Ulcerative Colitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Infectious Enterocolitis (e.g., Campylobacter, Salmonella) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: History of previous similar flares and known non-compliance with maintenance therapy, though stool cultures remain necessary to definitively exclude superimposed infection.

Ischemic Colitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient's young age, lack of vascular risk factors, and gradual rather than hyper-acute sudden onset of ischemia.

Diverticulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of massive gross bloody diarrhea, which is less common in uncomplicated diverticulitis compared to profound mucosal ulceration.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits signs of acute severe ulcerative colitis with profound gastrointestinal fluid and blood loss, evidenced by tachycardia, postural dizziness, and pallor. The presence of greater than six bloody stools a day coupled with systemic signs (fever, tachycardia) strictly meets criteria for severe disease. Assessing this patient urgently after-hours was critical to rule out complications like toxic megacolon or bowel perforation, and to intervene before hypovolemic shock could develop. Delaying care until the next routine appointment was clinically unsafe. The expected investigation results include continuous colonic mucosal inflammation with friability and ulceration extending proximally from the rectum on sigmoidoscopy that would assist in confirming the diagnosis at the hospital.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed to commence oral rehydration salts (ORS) aggressively. Withheld antidiarrheal agents (e.g., Loperamide) due to the risk of precipitating toxic megacolon. Prescribed Paracetamol 1000mg PO for pain and fever control; strictly advised against NSAIDs.
Positive Behavioral Change: To rebuild your hydration effectively, establish a routine of drinking one full glass of an oral rehydration solution immediately after every bowel movement. Once you successfully finish the glass, mark it on a piece of paper; seeing these checkmarks will reinforce your active role in avoiding a hospital admission for an IV drip. Let the avoidance of the emergency room be the direct reward for your consistent drinking habits.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent referral to Gastroenterology or Emergency Department if unable to maintain oral intake overnight. Otherwise, urgent GP review tomorrow for blood tests (FBC, CRP, UEC) and stool cultures.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop severe, constant, rigid abdominal pain, if your abdomen becomes suddenly very swollen, if you feel entirely completely unable to stand without fainting, or if the bleeding becomes continuous like a running tap.

